      "timestamp": "2026-09-25T11:58:12.578729Z",
      "user_text": "From the energy moments, find the high-temperature expansion (power series in $\\beta$) of the specific heat $C(\\beta)=\\beta^2\\,\\partial^2(\\ln Z/N)/\\partial\\beta^2$. This is NOT a literal Maxima command: the recursion/moment method has no generic CAS routine for it, so it is computed by this project's own spin-algebra module (`notebook/runtime/spin_algebra.py`) as an exact, deterministic closed-form calculation — the same graph-invariant energy moments $\\langle H^2\\rangle,\\langle H^3\\rangle,\\langle H^4\\rangle$ from the previous steps are converted to connected cumulants $\\kappa_n$ ($\\kappa_2=\\langle H^2\\rangle$, $\\kappa_4=\\langle H^4\\rangle-3\\langle H^2\\rangle^2$ since $\\langle H\\rangle=0$) and assembled into the power series $C(\\beta)=\\kappa_2\\beta^2-\\tfrac1{3!}\\kappa_3\\beta^3+\\tfrac1{2\\cdot2!}\\kappa_4\\beta^4+\\cdots$. For this $2\\times2$ lattice, $\\kappa_2=\\langle H^2\\rangle=\\tfrac14J^2$ and $\\kappa_4=\\tfrac14J^4-3(\\tfrac14J^2)^2=\\tfrac1{16}J^4$, giving the $\\beta^2$ and $\\beta^4$ coefficients below by hand — a cross-check anyone can redo, not a black box. Since this lattice has no triangles, no odd-order $\\beta^3$ term should appear.",
      "visible_action": "Memo",
      "operation": null,
      "operation_args": {},
      "backend": null,
      "rendered_command": null,
